.\" vim: ft=nroff
.TH APKOBBUNDLER 1 "August 2026" "apkobbundler" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
apkobbundler \- inspect and package a Unity OBB with an APK using REapk
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B python build.py
.RI [ options ]
.I input
.PP
.B python build.py
.I input
.BR --obb
.I obb
.BR --no-inject
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B apkobbundler
is the command-line program implemented by
.BR build.py
for inspecting an APK or XAPK/APKS bundle, locating a matching Unity expansion
file (OBB), and packaging that file into the APK using the native Python APIs
provided by REapk 0.1.1.
.PP
The program reads the package name and versionCode from the binary Android
manifest. It accepts OBB files named
.BI main.<versionCode>.<package>.obb
or
.BI patch.<versionCode>.<package>.obb .
When packaging is explicitly enabled, the OBB is stored as
.B assets/obb/<obb-filename>
and the resulting ZIP is rebuilt and signed with REapk's native APK Signature
Scheme v2+v3 implementation.
.PP
By default, the program refuses to produce an output APK after inspection.
This is intentional: REapk 0.1.1 can replace the body of an existing DEX
method, but does not expose a supported operation for declaring a new startup
class or method and updating DEX class_data. Therefore the current program
cannot safely inject the startup extraction hook required for a genuinely
standalone APK.
.PP
The
.B --no-inject
option enables the packaging step for testing or inspection only. Its output
is explicitly not standalone and does not extract the embedded OBB to the
Android expansion-file directory at application startup.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --obb \,path
Use the specified OBB instead of searching OBB members in an XAPK/APKS/ZIP
bundle. The basename must match the standard naming convention and must match
the APK package and versionCode.
.TP
.B --no-inject
Emit an APK containing the OBB asset without startup DEX injection. This
option is required to produce an output with the current implementation and
must not be interpreted as enabling automatic OBB extraction.
.TP
.BI --output \,path
Write the output APK to
.I path .
If omitted, use
.B output/<input-stem>-standalone.apk .
.TP
.BR -h , \--help
Show the command-line help and exit.
.SH INPUT HANDLING
For an ordinary
.BR .apk ,
the file itself is treated as the base APK and the OBB must be supplied with
.B --obb.
.PP
For
.BR .xapk ,
.BR .apks ,
and ZIP bundles, the program reads
.B manifest.json
when present, locates APK and OBB members, and selects the base APK. Bundles
containing multiple APKs that appear to be split, architecture, or
configuration APKs are rejected because REapk 0.1.1 does not merge splits.
.PP
If no explicit OBB is supplied, the program searches bundle members for a
basename matching
.BI main.<versionCode>.<package>.obb
or
.BI patch.<versionCode>.<package>.obb .
When both main and patch candidates are present, the main candidate is
preferred. The filename is validated against the manifest package and
versionCode, and an empty OBB is rejected.
.SH OUTPUT
With
.B --no-inject ,
the program writes a newly signed APK. The embedded OBB is stored without
compression under
.B assets/obb/ .
Existing stale JAR-signature metadata under
.B META-INF/
is removed before native signing.
.PP
The signing key and certificate are generated or reused by REapk under
.BR ~/.reapk/ .
Because this is not the original application's signing key, the output is
intended for clean installation and testing, not for updating an installation
signed by a different key.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The requested operation completed. With
.B --no-inject ,
this means only that the non-standalone packaging operation succeeded.
.TP
.B 2
Input validation failed, the OBB was not found or did not match, the bundle
contains unsupported splits, or standalone output was refused because startup
injection is unavailable.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect an APK and its separately supplied OBB:
.PP
.nf
python build.py input/game.apk --obb path/to/main.1435.com.example.game.obb
.fi
.PP
Package an XAPK for testing without startup injection:
.PP
.nf
python build.py input/game.xapk --no-inject
.fi
.PP
Write the non-standalone test artifact to a custom path:
.PP
.nf
python build.py input/game.apk \\
    --obb path/to/main.1435.com.example.game.obb \\
    --no-inject \\
    --output output/test.apk
.fi
.PP
Verify that the output is a readable ZIP and contains the embedded OBB:
.PP
.nf
python -m zipfile -t output/test.apk
python - <<'PY'
import zipfile
with zipfile.ZipFile("output/test.apk") as z:
    print([n for n in z.namelist() if n.startswith("assets/obb/")])
PY
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.B build.py
Python implementation of the command.
.TP
.B README.md
Project architecture, REapk API findings, setup instructions, limitations,
and verification notes.
.TP
.B ~/.reapk/signer.key
Cached private signing key managed by REapk. Protect this file.
.TP
.B ~/.reapk/signer.crt
Cached certificate corresponding to the REapk signing key.
.SH REQUIREMENTS
Python 3.10 or newer is recommended. Install REapk 0.1.1 in a virtual
environment:
.PP
.nf
python3 -m venv venv
. venv/bin/activate
python -m pip install 'reapk @ git+https://github.com/JRBusiness/REapk.git@v0.1.1'
.fi
.PP
The program does not require Java, apktool, smali, baksmali, zipalign,
apksigner, or keytool.
.SH LIMITATIONS
The current implementation does not inject code into an Application or launch
Activity, does not create a new Dalvik helper class, and does not copy an asset
to
.B /sdcard/Android/obb/<package>/
at application startup. Consequently, an output produced with
.B --no-inject
is not a standalone APK and must not be represented as one.
.PP
The program does not bypass LVL, Google Play licensing, DRM, authentication,
or signature checks. Use it only with software for which the operator has the
necessary authorization.
